<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagiellonian University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1364">1364</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as University of Kraków, Kraków Academy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jagiellonian University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> in 1364 and refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a>'s bequest in 1400. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> studied there in the 1490s. Under the partitions it remained a centre of Polish learning, especially in the Free City of Kraków and later in autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university.jpg" alt="Jagiellonian University" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Rachwał · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collegium_Maius_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> needed an educated class, above all lawyers who could arrange the country's laws and run its courts and offices. Pope Urban V allowed him to set up a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and a royal charter of foundation was issued on 12 May 1364, with a matching document from the city council granting privileges to the Studium Generale. After Casimir's death development stalled.</p><p>The university was restored in the 1390s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> and her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> chose to buy an existing building rather than build, and in 1399 acquired a house on Żydowska Street that had belonged to the Pęcherz family. The queen gave all her personal jewellery to the university, which allowed it to enrol 203 students. On 26 July 1400 Jagiełło reformed it on the model of the Sorbonne, and a Faculty of Theology was established.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For several centuries almost the whole intellectual elite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> studied there. Its astronomers, lawyers and theologians attracted eminent scholars, among them Albert Brudzewski, who from 1491 to 1495 was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>'s teachers. It was the first university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to set up independent chairs in mathematics and astronomy. Its golden age fell in the Polish Renaissance, between 1500 and 1535, when 3,215 students attended in the first decade of the 16th century. That record was not passed until the late 18th century.</p><p>After the third partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> became a free city under Austrian protection, and in 1846, after the Kraków Uprising, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>. The Austrians were in many ways hostile to the university but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-austria">Ferdinand I of Austria</a> decreed that it be kept. On 6 November 1939, after the German invasion, 184 professors were arrested and deported to Sachsenhausen concentration camp, and the university was closed for the rest of the war. It reopened in 1945, but was suppressed by the Communist government in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Jagiellonian Library, which holds Copernicus's De revolutionibus orbium coelestium, has almost 6.5 million volumes.  The university's alumni include Copernicus, King John III Sobieski and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>,. Today it is a public research university whose grounds form part of the Kraków Old Town, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jagiellonian University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Casimir III founded the <b>Kraków Academy</b>, now the Jagiellonian University.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the only place where Polish institutions functioned freely, and the <b>Jagiellonian University</b> was a centre of Polish culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-city-of-krakow">Free City of Krakow</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Casimir III the Great codified law, founded the <b>University of Krakow</b> in 1364 and added Red Ruthenia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385)</a></li></ul></section>
